From: pdx203 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: gpu: pin write-combined BO pages in the pmap; walk them only without the pin

Before every submission the write-combine re-assertion looked at every page of every scattered write-combined BO of the VM, whenever
one global epoch had moved (any client's allocation, GPU map or free anywhere) or 100 ms had passed. With the 768 MiB pool full
(usb-459: Dusklight, then Safari) that was 2 ms a walk on every other submission. The pages moved because XNU keeps one cache
attribute per physical page and a device-pager view's teardown sets it back to the default (vm_page_remove, also from the object
reaper later). The kernel now pins a page's write-combined attribute (patches/xnu zzzzzzzzzs-pmap-pinned-wc): NativeAdrenoSetPagesCacheMode
pins each page it makes write-combined and unpins it before restoring the default. Once a pin holds, no submission walks; a timed
sweep (1024 pages each 100 ms per VM) checks every BO and counts what it finds ([3]).

Without the pin (an older kernel) the walk is narrower than before: a BO at the next submission after its own CPU view changed
(BoCpuMap, BoCpuUnmap, a view made or torn down), every viewed BO each 100 ms ([2] counts what those find), and never-viewed BOs
in the sweep. The walk state is per VM (one static served every per-process VM); [4] counts pages walked.

